(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["cost",{"l:cost/how-do-you-predict-running-time-before-you-run-t":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Running time is (number of basic steps) x (time per step). The second factor depends on the machine; the first depends only on your code and the input size, so count it: \u003cb>add\u003c/b> sequential blocks, \u003cb>multiply\u003c/b> nested loops, \u003cb>sum\u003c/b> the inner counts of dependent loops, and count halvings with log2 n. Every built-in call that touches many elements (\u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code>, slicing, \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>) is a loop too, and must be counted.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At a meeting where everyone shakes hands with everyone else once, 5 people make 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10 handshakes, 100 people make 4,950, and 10,000 people make about 50 million. You need no stopwatch to know the last meeting is a disaster. A count written as a formula in n tells you how the cost \u003cb>grows\u003c/b>, and growth decides whether code survives real input sizes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Pick a cost model\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Decide what one step is: an arithmetic operation on a small integer, a comparison, an assignment, indexing \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, an average-case dict or set lookup. Each takes roughly constant time. Anything that touches many elements is \u003ci>not\u003c/i> one step; it costs one step per element it touches.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Combine the pieces\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Code shape\u003c/th>\u003cth>Steps\u003c/th>\u003cth>Growth\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two loops one after the other, n and m iterations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n + m\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n + m)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A loop of m inside a loop of n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n x m\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(nm)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>for i in range(n): for j in range(i + 1, n)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(n - 1) + ... + 1 + 0 = n(n - 1)/2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n^2)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>while n &gt; 1: n //= 2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>floor(log2 n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>i = 1\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>while i * i &lt;= n: i += 1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about sqrt(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(sqrt n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>for d in range(1, n + 1): for m in range(d, n + 1, d)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n/1 + n/2 + ... + n/n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>for i in range(n):\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>j = 1; while j &lt; n: j *= 2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n x ceil(log2 n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The dependent loop is a triangle, half of an n by n square, so it is still quadratic. The harmonic row deserves a second look: n/1 + n/2 + ... + n/n = n(1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n), and the bracket (the harmonic number) is about ln n + 0.58. A loop that visits the multiples of every d costs about n ln n, not n^2. Sieves have this shape.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Find the hidden loops\u003c/h5>\u003cp>One line can hide a loop: \u003ccode>x in a_list\u003c/code> scans up to the whole list, \u003ccode>a[i:j]\u003c/code> copies j - i references, \u003ccode>sum(a[:i])\u003c/code> copies and then adds, \u003ccode>s.count(c)\u003c/code> scans the string, \u003ccode>a.pop(0)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>a.insert(0, x)\u003c/code> shift every other element, \u003ccode>min(a)\u003c/code> scans everything. Put one of these inside a loop over n and you multiply by n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>To turn steps into seconds you need the price of a step. On a typical laptop, roughly:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>One iteration of a simple Python loop such as \u003ccode>total += i\u003c/code> costs about 30 to 100 ns. Plan with \u003cb>about 10^7 simple Python steps per second\u003c/b>; we measured just under 10^7 loop iterations per second on a busy machine.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A loop that runs inside C, in a built-in such as \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>str.join\u003c/code>, costs about 1 to 10 ns per element. We measured \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code> over 10^6 integers at about 9 ms, against about 50 ms for the same sum written as a Python \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>So with Python loops, 10^7 steps take about a second; n^2 with n = 10^4 is 10^8 steps, ten seconds or more; n log2 n with n = 10^6 is about 2 x 10^7 steps, a couple of seconds in pure Python and a fraction of a second when \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> does the work. The constraints table in the method module turns these prices into a target complexity for a given n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count before you code, every time: it takes a minute and rules out designs that cannot meet the constraints. It also locates the bottleneck, because the dominant term of the count is the line worth optimising. Counting tells you about growth; it cannot tell you which of two O(n) solutions is faster, nor how caches and C built-ins move the constant. For those questions, measure (the last card of this module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count how often the inner line of the dependent double loop runs for n = 5:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: j runs over 1, 2, 3, 4, so the inner line runs 4 times\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0    1    2    3    4\ninner  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  .    .    .    .      total 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: j runs over 2, 3, 4: three more\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0    1    2    3    4\ninner    4  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  .    .    .      total 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2: j runs over 3, 4: two more\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0    1    2    3    4\ninner    4    3  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  .    .      total 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: j = 4 only\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0    1    2    3    4\ninner    4    3    2  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  .      total 10\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: range(5, 5) is empty, so nothing runs\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i        0    1    2    3    4\ninner    4    3    2    1  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>    total 10 = 5 x 4 / 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The counts form a triangle, half an n by n square: n(n - 1)/2 = Theta(n^2)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 10        45 steps\nn = 1,000     499,500 steps\nn = 100,000   about 5 x 10^9 steps: many minutes in pure Python\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>These counting functions double as a reference card: each returns how often its innermost line runs, and the assertions check the closed forms in the comments.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\n\ndef dependent_pairs(n):          # n(n - 1)/2\n    steps = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            steps += 1\n    return steps\n\ndef halvings(n):                 # floor(log2 n) for n &gt;= 1\n    steps = 0\n    while n &gt; 1:\n        n //= 2\n        steps += 1\n    return steps\n\ndef multiples(n):                # n/1 + n/2 + ... + n/n, about n ln n\n    steps = 0\n    for d in range(1, n + 1):\n        for _ in range(d, n + 1, d):\n            steps += 1\n    return steps\n\nfor n in (1, 7, 64, 1000):\n    assert dependent_pairs(n) == n * (n - 1) // 2\n    assert halvings(n) == n.bit_length() - 1\n    assert multiples(n) == sum(n // d for d in range(1, n + 1))\nprint(multiples(10**5), round(10**5 * math.log(10**5)))   # 1166750 1151293\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hidden loops.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>if x in seen\u003c/code> where \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> is a list turns a single pass into O(n^2). Use a set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting the wrong size.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>for d in range(2, isqrt(v) + 1)\u003c/code> is O(sqrt v) in the \u003ci>value\u003c/i> v: about 10^9 steps for v = 10^18, although the input is a single number. Say what n measures.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assuming nested means quadratic.\u003c/b> A \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> inside a \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> whose pointer never moves backwards (two pointers, sliding windows, monotonic stacks) runs at most n times \u003ci>in total\u003c/i>. The amortised card shows why.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Big integers are not O(1).\u003c/b> Python ints grow without limit, and multiplying two numbers with thousands of digits costs far more than one step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slices in loops.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>for i in range(n): total = sum(a[:i])\u003c/code> copies i items each time: about n^2/2 steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How often does the body of \u003ccode>i = 1\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>while i &lt; n: i *= 3\u003c/code> run, as a formula? How many times for n = 100?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>i takes the values 1, 3, 9, 27, 81, ..., so the body runs once per power of 3 below n: ceil(log3 n) times for n &gt; 1. For n = 100 that is 5 times (i = 1, 3, 9, 27, 81). The growth is O(log n); the base only changes the constant factor.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A function loops over n words and, for each, checks \u003ccode>if w not in result\u003c/code> where \u003ccode>result\u003c/code> is a list of the unique words so far, then appends. What is the worst case, and what is the one-line fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When all n words are distinct, the k-th check scans k - 1 items: about n^2/2 comparisons, O(n^2). Keep a set beside the list (\u003ccode>seen = set()\u003c/code>, test \u003ccode>w not in seen\u003c/code>, then add to both): each check is O(1) on average, the loop is O(n), and the order of first appearance is kept.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\u003ccode>for i in range(1, n + 1):\u003c/code> with the body \u003ccode>j = i\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>while j &lt;= n: j += i\u003c/code>. Which row of the table is this, and what is its growth?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The harmonic row: for each i the inner loop runs floor(n / i) times, so the total is n(1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n), about n ln n. It is O(n log n), not O(n^2).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1732\u003c/b> Find the Highest Altitude: one pass with a running total. Expected: O(n) time, O(1) extra space; for gains [3, -4, 6, -1] the altitudes are 0, 3, -1, 5, 4 and the answer is 5.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2073\u003c/b> Time Needed to Buy Tickets: write the second-by-second simulation first and count its steps, then replace it with a count per person. Expected: the simulation costs about sum(tickets) steps, which depends on ticket values rather than n; the counting version is O(n). For tickets [4, 1, 3, 2] and k = 2 the answer is 9.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1588\u003c/b> Sum of All Odd Length Subarrays (stretch): count the three nested loops of the brute force before you optimise. Expected: brute force O(n^3) as written, O(n^2) with prefix sums, O(n) by counting how many odd-length subarrays contain each index; for [2, 5, 1, 4] the answer is 30.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count the steps of loops, dependent loops, halving loops and hidden built-in loops, and convert the count into a time estimate for Python.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/what-do-big-o-big-theta-and-big-omega-actually-p":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>f(n) = O(g(n)) means f grows \u003cb>at most\u003c/b> like g: beyond some n0, f(n) &lt;= c g(n) for a fixed constant c. Omega means \u003cb>at least\u003c/b>, and Theta means both, a tight bound. None of them means \"worst case\": the worst, best, average and expected costs are different functions, and each can be bounded with O, Omega or Theta. Keep every independent size (n, m, k, the output size) in the bound.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A bound is like a builder's quote. \"At most 10,000\" (O) promises the bill will not be higher, and it stays true if the bill is 50. \"At least 5,000\" (Omega) promises it will not be lower. \"Between 5,000 and 10,000\" (Theta) pins it down. \"At most a million\" is also true, just useless: binary search is technically O(n), but O(log n) is the statement that informs. Aim for the tightest bound you can justify, and say which situation it covers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The definitions\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>f(n) = O(g(n)) if there are constants c &gt; 0 and n0 such that f(n) &lt;= c g(n) for every n &gt;= n0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>f(n) = Omega(g(n)) if there are constants c &gt; 0 and n0 such that f(n) &gt;= c g(n) for every n &gt;= n0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>f(n) = Theta(g(n)) if both hold, possibly with different constants.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Example: f(n) = 3n^2 + 10n + 5. For n &gt;= 1, 10n &lt;= 10n^2 and 5 &lt;= 5n^2, so f(n) &lt;= 18n^2: O(n^2) with c = 18 and n0 = 1. Also f(n) &gt;= 3n^2 for every n, so Omega(n^2), and together Theta(n^2). Constant factors and lower-order terms disappear because one fixed multiple absorbs them.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Rules you use every day\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>Drop constant factors and lower-order terms: 5n log n + 100n = Theta(n log n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sum rule: O(f) + O(g) = O(max(f, g)). Product rule: a loop of O(f) iterations doing O(g) work each is O(f x g).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The base of a logarithm does not matter (log2 n = log10 n / log10 2, a constant factor), but the base of an exponential does: 4^n = (2^n)^2 is not O(2^n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The ladder: 1 &lt; log n &lt; sqrt n &lt; n &lt; n log n &lt; n^2 &lt; n^3 &lt; 2^n &lt; n!. Any power of log n grows more slowly than any positive power of n.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Several variables and the output\u003c/h5>\u003cp>When the input has several independent sizes, keep them all. Comparing two arrays of lengths n and m costs O(n + m) with sets and O(nm) with nested loops. A grid is O(rows x cols). A graph search is O(V + E), and E can reach V^2, so \"O(V)\" would be wrong. Keeping the k smallest of n items in a heap is O(n log k), far better than O(n log n) when k is small.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Output-sensitive\u003c/b> bounds include the size of the answer: listing every pair that sums to a target costs O(n + p) where p is the number of pairs reported, and p alone can be Theta(n^2). No algorithm beats the size of its own output.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Which case?\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Taken over\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Worst\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The hardest input of size n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linear search for a missing item: n comparisons\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Best\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The easiest input of size n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Insertion sort on sorted input: n - 1 comparisons\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Average\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inputs from an assumed distribution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linear search for an item at a uniformly random position: (n + 1)/2\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Expected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The algorithm's own random choices, for every input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quicksort with random pivots: about 1.39 n log2 n comparisons on any input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Amortised\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A whole sequence of operations, no probability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n appends to a dynamic array: O(n) in total\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Average and expected differ in what they assume. An average-case bound fails when real inputs do not look like the assumed distribution (already sorted input for a quicksort that always picks the first element). An expected bound holds for every input, because the randomness lives inside the algorithm. \"O(1) for a hash table\" is an average over keys that hash well, which an attacker can break; the hashing module shows how.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>What the classes mean at real sizes, at roughly 10^7 simple Python steps per second:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Growth\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 1,000\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^6\u003c/th>\u003cth>Python at n = 10^6\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>log2 n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>instant\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10^6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.1 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>n log2 n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>33\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10^4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 x 10^7\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>n^2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10^6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10^12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about a day\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2^n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 10^301\u003c/td>\u003ctd>unthinkable\u003c/td>\u003ctd>never\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Notice how little separates the classes at n = 10 and how much at n = 10^6: asymptotic notation is a statement about large n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003cb>Theta\u003c/b> when you can show the bound is tight; it is the most informative statement. Use \u003cb>O\u003c/b> when you only need a guarantee (\"no worse than\") and \u003cb>Omega\u003c/b> for lower bounds: any algorithm that must read all n inputs is Omega(n), and comparison sorting needs Omega(n log n) comparisons.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Always name the case. \"Insertion sort is Theta(n^2) in the worst case and Theta(n) in the best case\" is precise; \"insertion sort is Theta(n^2)\" is false for sorted input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Asymptotics compare growth, not speed at your n. Two O(n) solutions can differ by 10x, and an O(n log n) built-in written in C often beats an O(n) loop written in Python at n = 10^6. When the classes tie or n is small, measure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find where 3n^2 + 10n + 5 &lt;= 4n^2 starts to hold for good, which proves O(n^2) with c = 4:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 1: f(1) = 18 but 4n^2 = 4. Small n: the lower-order terms dominate\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n       1\nf(n)  \u003cmark>18\u003c/mark>\n4n^2    4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 5: f(5) = 130, 4n^2 = 100. Still above\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n       1    5\nf(n)   18  \u003cmark>130\u003c/mark>\n4n^2    4  100\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 10: f(10) = 405, 4n^2 = 400. Just above\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n       1    5    10\nf(n)   18  130  \u003cmark>405\u003c/mark>\n4n^2    4  100   400\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 11: f(11) = 478 &lt;= 484. From here on 4n^2 - f(n) = n^2 - 10n - 5 only grows\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n       1    5    10    11\nf(n)   18  130   405  \u003cmark>478\u003c/mark>\n4n^2    4  100   400   484\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">So c = 4, n0 = 11 works, and so does c = 18, n0 = 1. Any c &gt; 3 works with a big enough n0; c = 3 never does\u003c/p>\u003cpre>f(n) / n^2 at n = 10, 100, 1000:   4.05   3.1005   3.010005   -&gt; 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two numerical checks catch most mistakes: a ratio table (if f(n)/g(n) settles to a positive constant, f = Theta(g)) and a step counter run on best-case and worst-case inputs.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def ratio_table(f, g, sizes):\n    \"\"\"f(n)/g(n) for growing n: settles to a constant &gt; 0 means Theta(g),\n    heads to 0 means f grows slower, keeps growing means f grows faster.\"\"\"\n    return [(n, f(n) / g(n)) for n in sizes]\n\ndef insertion_sort_comparisons(a):\n    \"\"\"Sort a copy of a by insertion and return the number of comparisons.\"\"\"\n    a, comparisons = list(a), 0\n    for i in range(1, len(a)):\n        key, j = a[i], i - 1\n        while j &gt;= 0:\n            comparisons += 1\n            if a[j] &lt;= key:\n                break\n            a[j + 1] = a[j]\n            j -= 1\n        a[j + 1] = key\n    return comparisons\n\nn = 1000\nprint(insertion_sort_comparisons(range(n)))           # best case, sorted: 999\nprint(insertion_sort_comparisons(range(n, 0, -1)))    # worst case, reversed: 499500\nprint(ratio_table(lambda n: 3 * n * n + 10 * n + 5, lambda n: n * n, [10, 100, 1000]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"O means worst case.\"\u003c/b> O is an upper bound on whatever function you describe. Say \"worst-case O(n^2)\" or \"best-case Theta(n)\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Loose bounds passed off as tight.\u003c/b> \"Binary search is O(n)\" is true and misleading. Prefer Theta when you can show it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Collapsing variables.\u003c/b> O(n + m) is not O(n) when m can be far larger; in graphs E can reach V^2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hiding a big constant.\u003c/b> An algorithm doing 1000n steps loses to one doing n^2 steps for every n below 1000.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting what n measures.\u003c/b> Trial division up to sqrt(v) is polynomial in the value v but exponential in the number of digits of v.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">True or false, with a reason: (a) 2^(n+1) = O(2^n); (b) 4^n = O(2^n); (c) log2(n^3) = Theta(log n).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) True: 2^(n+1) = 2 x 2^n, a constant factor. (b) False: 4^n / 2^n = 2^n grows without bound, so no constant c works. (c) True: log2(n^3) = 3 log2 n, a constant factor.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A teammate writes \"quicksort is O(n log n)\" in a design note. When is that wrong, and how should it be phrased?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With a fixed pivot rule such as \"first element\", sorted or reversed input makes every split maximally unbalanced and the worst case is Theta(n^2). Precise versions: \"expected O(n log n) on every input with random pivots\", or \"worst case Theta(n^2), average Theta(n log n) over random inputs\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You must count how many of m query values occur in an array of n numbers. Give the cost of (a) \u003ccode>q in arr\u003c/code> for each query, (b) building a set once and looking each query up, (c) sorting once and binary searching each query. Which wins for n = 10^6 when m = 10, and when m = 10^5?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) O(nm), (b) O(n + m) expected, (c) O(n log n + m log n). For m = 10 all three are fine: (a) is ten C-level scans, about the cost of building the set. For m = 10^5, (a) is 10^11 comparisons and hopeless, while (b) stays O(n + m) and (c) about 2 x 10^7 steps. Only by keeping m in the bound can you see that the naive version is acceptable for tiny m and fatal for large m.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1351\u003c/b> Count Negative Numbers in a Sorted Matrix (derive): write three versions with three different costs before reading any hint. Expected: brute force O(mn); a binary search in each row O(m log n); a walk from one corner that discards a whole row or column at every step, O(m + n). For [[3, 2, -1], [1, -1, -2], [-1, -2, -3]] the answer is 6.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2215\u003c/b> Find the Difference of Two Arrays: state the cost with both sizes. Expected: nested membership tests O(n x m); sets O(n + m) expected. For [4, 1, 4, 7] and [7, 2, 2] the answer is [[1, 4], [2]] (each list in any order).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to state an honest, tight bound: which case it covers, which variables it depends on, and what O, Omega and Theta each promise.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/how-much-memory-does-a-program-really-use":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Space complexity counts the extra memory an algorithm needs as the input grows: \u003cb>auxiliary\u003c/b> space for variables, copies, buffers and the recursion stack, stated separately from the input and the output. An algorithm is \u003cb>in place\u003c/b> when its auxiliary space is O(1) (O(log n) for a recursion stack is usually accepted). In Python, count bytes too: a list stores 8-byte references, and every number it refers to is a separate object of 24 to 32 bytes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Working at a desk: the input is the pile of books on the shelf, the output is the report you hand in, and auxiliary space is the desk area you clear to work. Two people can write the same report, one with three sticky notes (O(1) extra), one by photocopying every book first (O(n) extra). When memory is the constraint, desk space is what you minimise.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>What to count\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Variables and buffers\u003c/b> you allocate: a set of seen values is O(n); a 26-slot letter count is O(1) because its size is fixed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Copies\u003c/b>: slicing (\u003ccode>a[1:]\u003c/code>), \u003ccode>sorted(a)\u003c/code> (a new list), \u003ccode>list(d)\u003c/code>, and every string operation, because strings are immutable and each \"change\" builds a new string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The recursion stack\u003c/b>: each active call keeps a frame alive. A recursion n levels deep uses O(n) space even if each call allocates nothing; balanced divide and conquer stays O(log n) deep.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The output\u003c/b>, stated separately: returning n strings needs O(n) space whatever you do, so the interesting figure is the auxiliary space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Count what is alive \u003cb>at the same moment\u003c/b> (the peak), not everything ever allocated. A loop that builds and drops a temporary list of size n on each of n iterations costs O(n^2) time but only O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Reuse instead of copy\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The main way to save space is to keep only the state that later steps still need. A grid computation that looks only at the previous row keeps one row, not the whole table (a \u003cb>rolling array\u003c/b>, traced below). An in-place algorithm overwrites positions once they are no longer needed (reversing with two indices, compacting with a write pointer). A generator yields items one at a time instead of building a list.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Python's real bytes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Measured with \u003ccode>sys.getsizeof\u003c/code> on 64-bit CPython 3.12:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Object\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bytes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One reference inside a list or tuple\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Empty list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>56 (plus 8 per slot, including spare capacity)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>int\u003c/code> below 2^30 in size\u003c/td>\u003ctd>28 (values -5 to 256 are shared, cached objects)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>int\u003c/code> near 2^100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>float\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>24\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tuple of two items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>56\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Empty dict / empty set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64 / 216\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>So \u003ccode>list(range(10**6))\u003c/code> needs about 8 MB of references plus about 28 MB of int objects; \u003ccode>tracemalloc\u003c/code> reported 40 MB in our run. The same numbers in an \u003ccode>array.array(\"q\")\u003c/code> took 8.2 MB, and a NumPy int64 array takes 8 MB. A 10^4 x 10^4 table stored as nested Python lists holds 10^8 references, 800 MB before counting a single distinct int object: a table of that shape must be rolled or vectorised.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two examples, with the space derived step by step:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>sum([x * x for x in range(n)])\u003c/code> builds the whole list before adding: O(n) auxiliary, a 40 MB peak for n = 10^6 in our run. \u003ccode>sum(x * x for x in range(n))\u003c/code> streams one value at a time: O(1) auxiliary, a peak under 1 kB.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>def total(a): return 0 if not a else a[0] + total(a[1:])\u003c/code> goes n levels deep, and level k holds its own slice of n - k items. At the deepest point all of them are alive: n + (n - 1) + ... + 1, about n^2/2 references, so O(n^2) space and O(n^2) time for the copying. Passing an index instead of a slice makes it O(n) stack and O(n) time; a loop makes it O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Check space whenever n times the per-item size approaches the memory limit (online judges often allow 256 MB; a list of 10^7 distinct Python ints is about 400 MB), whenever recursion depth can grow with n, and whenever data is streamed (logs, files, sockets) so that holding it all is not an option. Do not chase O(1) space at the cost of time or clarity when memory is plentiful: a set that makes the code O(n) is usually the right trade against an O(1)-space O(n^2) scan.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the right-or-down paths from the top-left to the bottom-right of a 3 x 4 grid, keeping a single row. Invariant: while row r is being built, cells left of j already hold row r and cells from j on still hold row r - 1, which is exactly what the update \u003ccode>row[j] = row[j] + row[j - 1]\u003c/code> needs (from above, plus from the left).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 1: one way to reach each cell (keep moving right)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1  1  1  1 ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 2, j = 1: from above (old row[1] = 1) plus from the left (new row[0] = 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> 1  1 ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 2, j = 2: 1 + 2 = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1  2 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> 1 ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 2, j = 3: 1 + 3 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1  2  3 \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 3, j = 1: 2 + 1 = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> 3  4 ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row 3, j = 2 and j = 3: 3 + 3 = 6, then 4 + 6 = 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1  3 \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark> ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Answer 10, which is C(5, 2), using 4 cells instead of a 12-cell table: O(cols) space, O(rows x cols) time\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row  [ 1  3  6 \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark> ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import tracemalloc\n\ndef peak_bytes(fn, *args):\n    \"\"\"Peak bytes held by Python objects allocated while fn(*args) runs.\"\"\"\n    tracemalloc.start()\n    try:\n        fn(*args)\n        return tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()[1]\n    finally:\n        tracemalloc.stop()\n\ndef grid_paths(rows, cols):\n    \"\"\"Right/down paths through a rows x cols grid, keeping one row: O(cols) space.\"\"\"\n    row = [1] * cols\n    for _ in range(rows - 1):\n        for j in range(1, cols):\n            row[j] += row[j - 1]\n    return row[-1]\n\nprint(grid_paths(3, 4))                                              # 10\nprint(peak_bytes(lambda n: sum([x * x for x in range(n)]), 10**6))   # about 40 million\nprint(peak_bytes(lambda n: sum(x * x for x in range(n)), 10**6))     # a few hundred\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the stack.\u003c/b> A recursion n levels deep is O(n) space, and in Python it also hits the default recursion limit near 1,000.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slicing in recursion.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>f(a[1:])\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>f(a[:mid])\u003c/code> copy; pass \u003ccode>lo, hi\u003c/code> indices instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keeping references alive.\u003c/b> Appending every intermediate result to a list \"for debugging\" turns O(1) space into O(n) or worse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting the output as waste.\u003c/b> If the answer has n items, O(n) space is unavoidable; report the auxiliary space separately.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>.sort()\u003c/code>.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sorted(a)\u003c/code> allocates a new list; \u003ccode>a.sort()\u003c/code> sorts in place, though Timsort can still use up to n/2 temporary references while merging.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the auxiliary space of \u003ccode>def has_neg(a): return bool(a) and (a[0] &lt; 0 or has_neg(a[1:]))\u003c/code> on a list with no negatives, and of a version that passes an index?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The slice version keeps up to n frames alive and frame k holds a slice of n - k items, so about n^2/2 references are alive at the deepest point: O(n^2) space (and O(n^2) time for the copies). With \u003ccode>has_neg(a, i)\u003c/code> each frame holds O(1), so the stack is O(n); a plain loop needs O(1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Merge sort and heapsort both take O(n log n) time. Which needs only O(1) auxiliary space, and what does the other need?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Heapsort rearranges the array in place: O(1) auxiliary. Merge sort needs an O(n) buffer for merging plus an O(log n) stack, which is the price of its stability.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A computation over two strings of length 10^4 fills a table where each row reads only the previous row. Estimate the memory of the full table as nested Python lists, and of two rolling rows.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The full table has 10^8 cells: 800 MB of references alone, and up to about 3.6 GB if the cells hold distinct int objects. Two rows of 10^4 cells are about 160 kB of references plus the ints, under 1 MB. Rolling turns impossible into trivial.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>119\u003c/b> Pascal's Triangle II: build row k in a single list of length k + 1. Expected: O(k^2) time and O(k) extra space; rowIndex 5 gives [1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1]. If your one-list version produces wrong numbers, compare the direction of your inner loop with the invariant in the worked trace: which old value does each update still need?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>557\u003c/b> Reverse Words in a String III: reverse each word, keep the word order. Expected: O(n) time; the output needs O(n) space because strings are immutable, and the list of reversed pieces is O(n) auxiliary; \"deep sea fish\" becomes \"peed aes hsif\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count auxiliary space, including the stack and hidden copies, and estimate the real memory of Python data.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/why-is-list-append-o-1-when-a-resize-copies-the-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>amortised\u003c/b> bound limits the total cost of any sequence of n operations, then divides by n. A dynamic array that doubles when full copies 1 + 2 + 4 + ... &lt; 2n elements during n appends, so each append is O(1) amortised although a single append can cost O(n). No probability is involved: the bound holds for every sequence. The same accounting, \"each element is moved only a constant number of times\", explains two-stack queues, binary counters, sliding windows and monotonic stacks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An annual rail pass costs a lot once, but spread over every journey of the year it is cheap per trip. Amortised analysis does the same accounting for code: a rare expensive operation is paid for by the many cheap ones around it, provided the expensive one cannot happen too often.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Aggregate method: add up, then divide\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A dynamic array keeps \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> items in a block of \u003ccode>capacity\u003c/code> slots. Appending into a free slot costs one write. Appending when full first allocates a block twice as big and copies all \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> items across. Starting from capacity 1, copies happen when the size reaches 1, 2, 4, ..., up to the largest power of two below n, so the copies total 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 2^k &lt; 2^(k+1) &lt;= 2n. Adding the n writes, n appends cost under 3n steps: \u003cb>O(1) amortised per append\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Credit (banker's) method: pay ahead\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Charge every append 3 coins: one pays for its own write and two are saved with the new item. When a block of capacity c fills up, the c/2 items added since the last resize hold c coins between them, exactly enough to copy all c items. The bank never goes negative, so 3 coins per append pays for everything, including the copies.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Any constant growth factor works; a constant increment does not\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Growing by a factor r &gt; 1 costs about 1/(r - 1) copies per append on average: about 1 for r = 2 and 2 for r = 1.5. Growing by a fixed number of slots does not work: with +16 the copies are 16 + 32 + 48 + ..., about n^2/32 in total, roughly 3,100 copies per append at n = 10^5 in our simulation, and growing linearly with n.\u003c/p>\u003cp>CPython grows lists by about one eighth: \u003ccode>new_allocated = (newsize + (newsize &gt;&gt; 3) + 6) &amp; ~3\u003c/code>. Reading capacities from \u003ccode>sys.getsizeof\u003c/code> while appending reproduces the comment in the source: 0, 4, 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 52, 64, 76, 92. A factor of about 1.125 means at most about 8 copies per append on average (our simulation of the rule gave 8.0 at 10^5 appends and 8.5 at 10^6), often fewer because \u003ccode>realloc\u003c/code> can sometimes extend the block in place. The payoff is memory: just after a resize at most about 11% of the slots are spare, against up to 50% with doubling.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Shrinking without thrashing\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If an array halved its capacity as soon as it fell to half full, alternating one append and one pop at that boundary would resize every time: O(n) per operation. The fix is a gap: shrink only when the array is a quarter full, so Theta(n) cheap operations separate any two resizes. CPython reallocates a list smaller only when its size falls below half the capacity, and keeps an eighth of slack when it does, so there is room to move in both directions.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Two stacks make a queue\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Push onto \u003ccode>inbox\u003c/code>. To pop, if \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code> is empty, move everything from \u003ccode>inbox\u003c/code> onto \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code> (which reverses the order, so the oldest item ends on top), then pop from \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code>. One pop can move n items, but each item is pushed onto inbox once, moved once and popped once: a constant number of stack operations per item, so n queue operations cost O(n) in total.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The binary counter\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Incrementing a binary counter flips the trailing 1s to 0 and one 0 to 1. A single increment can flip log2 n bits, but bit 0 flips on every increment, bit 1 on every second, bit i on every 2^i-th: n + n/2 + n/4 + ... &lt; 2n flips in total. Increments 1 to 8 flip 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 4 bits: 15 in all, below 16.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>\"Each element enters and leaves once\"\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The same argument explains loops that look quadratic. In a sliding window the left edge only moves right, so the inner \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> that advances it runs at most n times over the whole scan, not n times per step. In a monotonic stack every element is pushed once and popped at most once. Count the total over the run, not the worst single iteration.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Doubling array: n appends cost fewer than 3n element writes including copies, O(1) amortised; one append is O(n) in the worst case. In our run, 10^5 appends made 131,071 copies, 1.31 per append.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two-stack queue: n operations cost at most 4n stack operations; a single pop can cost O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Binary counter: n increments flip fewer than 2n bits (199,994 for n = 10^5); a single increment can flip O(log n).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reach for amortised analysis whenever an operation is usually cheap but occasionally rebuilds, refills or resizes, and whenever a nested loop's inner pointer never moves backwards. Do \u003cb>not\u003c/b> rely on an amortised bound when each operation has its own deadline: a request that happens to trigger a 1 GB resize is slow however good the average is. That is why Redis rehashes its hash tables a few buckets at a time during normal operations, and why Go splits each map into tables of at most 1,024 entries, so that a single growth step copies at most 1,024 entries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Nine appends into a doubling array that starts with capacity 1:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append a: a slot is free\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [\u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>]\ncapacity 1, copied 0, total copies 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append b: full, so allocate 2 slots and copy 1 item first\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>]\ncapacity 2, copied 1, total copies 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append c: full, allocate 4 and copy 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a b \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> .]\ncapacity 4, copied 2, total copies 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append d: a slot is free, no copy\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a b c \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>]\ncapacity 4, copied 0, total copies 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append e: full, allocate 8 and copy 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a b c d \u003cmark>e\u003c/mark> . . .]\ncapacity 8, copied 4, total copies 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append f, g, h: three cheap appends fill the block\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a b c d e \u003cmark>f g h\u003c/mark>]\ncapacity 8, copied 0, total copies 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">append i: full, allocate 16 and copy 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots [a b c d e f g h \u003cmark>i\u003c/mark> . . . . . . .]\ncapacity 16, copied 8, total copies 15\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">After 9 appends: 15 copies + 9 writes = 24 &lt;= 3 x 9. Each expensive append doubles the room for cheap ones\u003c/p>\u003cpre>copies so far: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 &lt; 2 x 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class DynamicArray:\n    \"\"\"A list-like array that doubles its capacity when full and counts element copies.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.capacity, self.size = 1, 0\n        self.slots = [None]\n        self.copies = 0\n\n    def append(self, x):\n        if self.size == self.capacity:            # full: move everything to a bigger block\n            self.capacity *= 2\n            bigger = [None] * self.capacity\n            for i in range(self.size):\n                bigger[i] = self.slots[i]\n            self.copies += self.size\n            self.slots = bigger\n        self.slots[self.size] = x\n        self.size += 1\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        if not 0 &lt;= i &lt; self.size:\n            raise IndexError(i)\n        return self.slots[i]\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return self.size\n\n\nclass TwoStackQueue:\n    \"\"\"FIFO queue from two stacks: amortised O(1) per operation.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self.outbox:                       # refill only when empty\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.inbox) + len(self.outbox)\n\n\narr = DynamicArray()\nfor x in range(100_000):\n    arr.append(x)\nprint(arr.copies)                                 # 131071, below 2 x 100000\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Amortised is not average.\u003c/b> There is no probability: the bound holds for every sequence, but it limits the total, not each operation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The slow operation still happens.\u003c/b> Latency-sensitive code (games, trading, request handlers) may need incremental resizing, or preallocation such as \u003ccode>[None] * n\u003c/code> when the size is known.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Additive growth.\u003c/b> Growing by a fixed number of slots makes appends O(n) amortised.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shrinking at half full\u003c/b> makes alternating push and pop thrash.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Replaying the expensive state.\u003c/b> If a structure can be copied, undone or persisted, an adversary can force the \"rare\" expensive step again and again from the same state, and the amortised bound no longer holds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Refilling too eagerly.\u003c/b> In a two-stack queue, moving items into \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code> while it still holds items breaks FIFO order; refill only when it is empty.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Continue the trace: after 17 appends to the doubling array, what are the capacity and the total number of copies? Is it below 2n?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 17th append finds the array full at 16 items, allocates 32 slots and copies 16, so capacity 32 and total copies 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31, below 2 x 17 = 34.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can no element move from inbox to outbox twice in a two-stack queue, and why does that give amortised O(1)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Once an element is in outbox it only leaves by being popped as the front of the queue; nothing ever moves it back to inbox. So each element is pushed once, moved once and popped once: at most 4 stack operations per element over its lifetime, so n queue operations cost O(n) in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague grows a buffer by 1,000 slots each time it fills. Roughly how many copies do 10^6 appends make, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Copies happen at sizes 1,000, 2,000, ..., 999,000: 1,000 x (1 + 2 + ... + 999), about 5 x 10^8 copies, or about 500 per append, and the figure grows in proportion to n. Grow by a constant factor (x 1.5 or x 2) instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many bits do increments 1 to 16 of a binary counter flip in total, and why is it below 32?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>31. Bit 0 flips 16 times, bit 1 eight times, bit 2 four, bit 3 twice and bit 4 once (when the counter reaches 16). In general bit i flips floor(n / 2^i) times, so the total is below n(1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ...) = 2n.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1656\u003c/b> Design an Ordered Stream: a pointer that only ever moves forward. Expected: O(n) total work for n inserts, so O(1) amortised per insert, even though a single insert can return a long run of values; with n = 3, inserting ids 3, 1, 2 (values \"c\", \"a\", \"b\") returns [], then [\"a\"], then [\"b\", \"c\"].\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1381\u003c/b> Design a Stack With Increment Operation (stretch): first write \u003ccode>increment(k, val)\u003c/code> as a loop over the bottom k items, then make it O(1). Expected: every operation O(1) in the worst case, not just amortised, by storing pending increments lazily; push 1, push 2, increment(2, 5), pop, pop returns 7 then 6.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to prove that a sequence of operations is cheap per operation even when single operations are expensive, and when that guarantee is not enough.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/why-does-scanning-an-array-beat-chasing-pointers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The word-RAM model behind Big-O charges every memory access one step. That is right for growth and wrong by up to 100x in practice: a value already in the L1 cache arrives in about a nanosecond, one in main memory in about 100. Memory moves in 64-byte \u003cb>cache lines\u003c/b> and the hardware prefetches sequential lines, so code that walks memory in order (arrays, row by row) runs several times faster than code that jumps around (linked nodes, column by column, shuffled order) with exactly the same operation count.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A library with a reading desk: fetching a book from the stacks takes minutes, but each trip brings back the whole shelf section. If the next books you need are neighbours on that shelf, they are already on your desk. Reading in shelf order costs one trip per section; reading in random order costs one trip per book. Same number of books, very different afternoon.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The model and the machine\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Algorithm analysis uses the \u003cb>word-RAM\u003c/b>: memory is one huge array of machine words, and reading or writing any address costs one step. That is why \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> is O(1): the address is the base address plus i times the element size, one multiplication and one load. Real hardware adds small, fast copies of recently used memory close to each core:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical size\u003c/th>\u003cth>Latency, roughly\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>L1 cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32 to 64 kB per core\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 ns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>L2 cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.5 to 2 MB per core\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 to 5 ns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>L3 cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>tens of MB, shared\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 to 40 ns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Main memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GBs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80 to 100 ns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SSD random read\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TBs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 100 µs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>One miss to main memory costs as much as about a hundred additions. Sequential streams are different: the prefetcher recognises the pattern and loads lines before you ask, so a scan runs at bandwidth (tens of GB/s) rather than paying the latency for every line.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Cache lines and locality\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Memory moves in 64-byte lines on most x86 and ARM processors (Apple's M-series chips use 128 bytes). Reading one 8-byte value brings its seven neighbours along. \u003cb>Spatial locality\u003c/b> means using those neighbours next; \u003cb>temporal locality\u003c/b> means reusing data while it is still cached.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Contiguous versus linked\u003c/h5>\u003cp>An array keeps its elements side by side: a scan touches each line once, in order. A linked list keeps nodes wherever the allocator put them, and each \u003ccode>node.next\u003c/code> is a load from an address the CPU cannot know until the previous load finishes, so misses cannot overlap.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>What this means in Python\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A Python list is a contiguous array of \u003cb>references\u003c/b>; the objects live elsewhere on the heap. Objects created one after another usually sit close together, so scanning a freshly built list is fairly cache-friendly, but shuffle the list and the same objects are visited in random address order. \u003ccode>array.array\u003c/code> and NumPy store raw values contiguously, 8 bytes per float with no object headers, which is smaller and faster to scan, and NumPy's loops run in C. A nested list \u003ccode>grid[i][j]\u003c/code> is a list of separate row lists, so walking row by row stays within one row's references at a time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Measured on a typical laptop, roughly (CPython 3.12, NumPy 1.26), with identical operation counts in each pair:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Experiment\u003c/th>\u003cth>In order\u003c/th>\u003cth>Out of order\u003c/th>\u003cth>Ratio\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sum\u003c/code> over 10^7 floats: list in creation order vs the same objects shuffled\u003c/td>\u003ctd>138 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>458 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.3x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The same at 10^6 floats\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7.5 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>18 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.4x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>NumPy 4000 x 4000: sum each row slice vs each column slice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>33 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>122 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.7x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Nested lists 2000 x 2000, \u003ccode>grid[i][j]\u003c/code>: row-major vs column-major double loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>524 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>872 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.7x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Python loop over a list of 10^6 ints vs walking 10^6 linked \u003ccode>Node\u003c/code> objects\u003c/td>\u003ctd>58 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>109 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.9x\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two lessons hide in the table. The gap grows with size, because small data fits in cache whatever the order. And pure-Python loops show smaller gaps, because tens of nanoseconds of interpreter work per step hide part of each miss; C loops, where the work per element is tiny, expose the full cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Think about locality when the data is bigger than the caches (more than a few MB), when a loop is hot, and when choosing between structures with equal Big-O: prefer arrays to linked lists, traverse 2D data in the order it is stored (row by row for nested lists and C-ordered NumPy arrays), and keep the fields you scan together in their own array (a struct of arrays). On disk the same idea becomes B-trees with wide nodes and log files written sequentially. Do not tune access order for loops over a few thousand items: it all fits in cache, and in Python choosing a C built-in matters far more.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A 4 x 8 matrix of 8-byte floats stored row by row, so each row fills exactly one 64-byte line. Pretend the cache holds only 2 lines (real caches hold thousands, but a large matrix overflows them in the same way):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Row-major, read M[0][0]: miss, so line 0 (all of row 0) is loaded\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ \u003cmark>row0\u003c/mark>  -   ]     reads 1, misses 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">M[0][1] to M[0][7] are all in line 0: seven hits\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ row0  -   ]         reads 8, misses 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">M[1][0]: miss, load row 1, then seven hits\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ row0 \u003cmark>row1\u003c/mark> ]     reads 16, misses 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rows 2 and 3 the same way: one miss per eight reads\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ \u003cmark>row2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>row3\u003c/mark> ] reads 32, misses 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Column-major instead, read M[0][0] then M[1][0]: two misses fill the cache\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ \u003cmark>row0\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>row1\u003c/mark> ] reads 2, misses 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">M[2][0] and M[3][0]: two more misses evict rows 0 and 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ \u003cmark>row2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>row3\u003c/mark> ] reads 4, misses 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">M[0][1]: row 0 was evicted, so it misses again; every column-order read misses\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cache [ \u003cmark>row0\u003c/mark> row3 ]     reads 5, misses 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Column-major total: 32 reads and 32 misses, against 4 misses row-major. Same Big-O, 8 times the memory traffic\u003c/p>\u003cpre>row-major 4 misses     column-major 32 misses\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nimport timeit\n\ndef order_effect(n=10**6, seed=1):\n    \"\"\"Time sum() over the same float objects in creation order and in shuffled order.\"\"\"\n    in_order = [float(i) for i in range(n)]\n    shuffled = in_order[:]\n    random.Random(seed).shuffle(shuffled)\n    t_in = min(timeit.repeat(lambda: sum(in_order), number=5, repeat=5)) / 5\n    t_sh = min(timeit.repeat(lambda: sum(shuffled), number=5, repeat=5)) / 5\n    return t_in, t_sh\n\nt_in, t_sh = order_effect()\nprint(f\"in order {t_in * 1e3:.1f} ms, shuffled {t_sh * 1e3:.1f} ms, ratio {t_sh / t_in:.1f}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting Big-O alone when classes tie.\u003c/b> Two O(n) traversals can differ by 3x or more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Column loops over row-major data\u003c/b>: nested lists, C-ordered NumPy arrays, images.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Linked structures in Python.\u003c/b> Each node is a full object and each step a dependent load plus interpreter work; a list or a deque is almost always faster.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Benchmarking tiny inputs.\u003c/b> Everything fits in L1 or L2, so locality effects vanish; test at realistic sizes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tuning order before choosing tools.\u003c/b> In Python, moving the loop into C (\u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, NumPy) usually matters more than access order. Measure first.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A NumPy array of shape (4000, 4000) is stored in C order. Which is faster, \u003ccode>sum(M[i, :].sum() for i in range(4000))\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>sum(M[:, j].sum() for j in range(4000))\u003c/code>, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The row version. Each row slice is 4000 contiguous floats streamed line by line; each column slice reads one float from each of 4000 rows, 32,000 bytes apart, so almost every element needs its own cache line. We measured about 33 ms against 122 ms.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do databases index data with B-trees holding hundreds of keys per node rather than with binary search trees?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each node visit costs one expensive transfer (a disk page, or a run of cache lines), whereas comparisons inside a node are cheap and contiguous. With B keys per node the height is about log_B n instead of log2 n: for n = 10^9 and B = 500, about 4 levels instead of 30, so about 4 transfers per lookup.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You store 10^6 points and repeatedly need the mean x. Compare a list of \u003ccode>(x, y)\u003c/code> tuples with two \u003ccode>array('d')\u003c/code> arrays for memory and scan speed.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The tuple list holds 10^6 references (8 MB), 10^6 tuples (56 bytes each) and 2 x 10^6 float objects (24 bytes each): about 112 MB, with the x values scattered across the heap. Two arrays hold raw doubles side by side: 16 MB, and the mean of x reads 8 MB sequentially. The struct of arrays wins on both counts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>No tracker problem drills this directly; run the experiment instead.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>order_effect()\u003c/code> from the template at n = 10^5, 10^6 and 10^7 (the last needs about 400 MB; use 3 x 10^6 if memory is tight). Expected: a ratio near 1.5 at 10^5, roughly 2 to 3 at 10^6 and roughly 3 to 4 at 10^7, growing as the data outgrows the caches.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Time the row-slice and column-slice sums of a 4000 x 4000 NumPy array, and \u003ccode>M.sum()\u003c/code>. Expected: column slices roughly 3 to 4 times slower than row slices, and \u003ccode>M.sum()\u003c/code> faster than both.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why memory access order changes speed at equal Big-O, and how to lay out and traverse data so that it streams through the caches.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/what-do-python-s-built-in-operations-really-cost":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Python hides loops inside innocent-looking operations. Indexing, \u003ccode>len\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>append\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop()\u003c/code> from the end and dict or set lookups are O(1) (amortised, or on average); \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code>, slicing, \u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code> and string concatenation are O(n). Built-ins run their loops in C, roughly 5 to 50 times faster per element than a Python loop. Choose the container by the operations you need, and keep hidden O(n) calls out of loops.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A shop has a price list. Paying item by item at the till is a Python loop; a bulk order processed in the warehouse is a built-in like \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, done at machine speed. Knowing the price list lets you find the one line that turns a sub-second program into a ten-minute one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The cost sheet (CPython; n is the container's length)\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Structure\u003c/th>\u003cth>O(1)\u003c/th>\u003cth>O(log n)\u003c/th>\u003cth>O(n) or more\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a[i] = x\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>len\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>append\u003c/code> (amortised), \u003ccode>pop()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x in a\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>insert(i, x)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pop(i)\u003c/code> (O(n - i)), \u003ccode>a[i:j]\u003c/code> (O(j - i)), \u003ccode>a + b\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>min\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code> is O(n log n), O(n) on sorted or reversed input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>dict\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>set\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>get, set, \u003ccode>in\u003c/code>, delete (on average)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>iteration and copy; any single operation in the worst case; \u003ccode>s &amp; t\u003c/code> is O(min(len s, len t)), \u003ccode>s | t\u003c/code> O(len s + len t)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>append\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>appendleft\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>popleft\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>d[0]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>d[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>d[i]\u003c/code> near the middle, \u003ccode>x in d\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>rotate(k)\u003c/code> is O(k)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>str\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>len\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s + t\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>s[i:j]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>t in s\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>find\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>split\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> (total length); building by \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> in a loop is quadratic in general\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code> on a list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>h[0]\u003c/code> (the minimum)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heappush\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>heappop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>heapreplace\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapify\u003c/code> O(n); \u003ccode>nsmallest(k, xs)\u003c/code> O(n log k)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> on a sorted list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>insort\u003c/code>: O(log n) to find the place, then O(n) to shift\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Why each surprise happens\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A list is one contiguous block of references\u003c/b> with spare room at the end (the amortised card). The end is cheap; the front is not: \u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> move every other reference by one slot. That is a fast \u003ccode>memmove\u003c/code>, but still n moves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A deque is a doubly linked list of 64-slot blocks.\u003c/b> Both ends are O(1) and nothing else moves; the price is that \u003ccode>d[i]\u003c/code> must walk block by block towards the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>dict and set are hash tables\u003c/b>: O(1) on average when hashing spreads keys well (the hashing module shows when it does not). A \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> caches its hash, so repeated lookups with the same string object do not rehash it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Strings and bytes are immutable\u003c/b>, so \u003ccode>s + t\u003c/code> builds a new object of length len(s) + len(t). CPython has a special case: when \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> is a \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> with no other references, \u003ccode>s += t\u003c/code> may resize it in place, so a loop of \u003ccode>s += piece\u003c/code> is often linear in practice. The trick is fragile: a second reference to \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>, a \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code> object, or another Python implementation such as PyPy makes the loop quadratic, and both the docs and PEP 8 tell you not to rely on it. Collect pieces in a list and \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code> them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code>\u003c/b> finds the slot in O(log n) and then inserts into a list: O(n). Inserting n items one at a time is O(n^2); a heap, or one sort at the end, is O(n log n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Built-ins run in C.\u003c/b> A Python-level step costs tens of nanoseconds; the same step inside \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>str.join\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>set(...)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Counter(...)\u003c/code> costs a few. An O(n log n) \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> can beat an O(n) Python loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Integers have arbitrary precision.\u003c/b> Arithmetic on small ints is O(1); on numbers with thousands of digits it is not.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Measured on a typical laptop, roughly (CPython 3.12):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> with 10^6 items, value absent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x in set\u003c/code> with 10^6 items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 to 40 ns, about 400,000 times faster\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>d[k]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 50 ns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sum(a)\u003c/code> over 10^6 ints, and the same as a Python \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>9 ms and 50 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> on 10^6 random floats, and on already sorted ones\u003c/td>\u003ctd>620 ms and 29 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Drain 10^5 items with \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>, and with \u003ccode>deque.popleft()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.7 s and 5 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^5 calls of \u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code>, and of \u003ccode>heapq.heappush\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.6 s and 13 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>bytes +=\u003c/code> 10^5 times, and 2 x 10^5 times\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.31 s and 1.43 s (quadratic)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>str +=\u003c/code> 4 x 10^5 times on a local, and \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code> of the same pieces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 ms and 17 ms\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>str +=\u003c/code> 4 x 10^4 times while a list also holds every partial string\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.94 s (quadratic)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>You need\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\u003cth>Avoid\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Membership tests, de-duplication\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>set\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dict\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> inside a loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>FIFO queue, BFS frontier, sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>append\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pop()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The smallest or largest, again and again\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>min(a)\u003c/code> in a loop, \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted data, many queries, few inserts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a sorted \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted data with many inserts and deletes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sortedcontainers.SortedList\u003c/code> (third party)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> for large n\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>collections.Counter\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list.count\u003c/code> in a loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Building text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a list of pieces and one \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>, or on a shared \u003ccode>str\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Why \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> on a list costs O(n) but \u003ccode>popleft()\u003c/code> on a deque costs O(1):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A list stores its references in one block, with index 0 at the start\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list   [ \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark> b c d e f ]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">list.pop(0) returns a, then shifts the other five references one slot left\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list   [ \u003cmark>b c d e f\u003c/mark> . ]          5 moves\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop(0) again: four moves. Draining n items moves (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 0, so O(n^2)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list   [ \u003cmark>c d e f\u003c/mark> . . ]          4 moves\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A deque of a..f: one 64-slot block, items in slots 32 to 37, leftindex = 32\u003c/p>\u003cpre>block  [ ... \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark> b c d e f ... ]  leftindex 32\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">deque.popleft() returns a and only increments leftindex: no item moves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>block  [ ... . \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark> c d e f ... ]  leftindex 33\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">popleft() again: leftindex 34. When a block empties it is unlinked, still O(1); draining n items is O(n)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>block  [ ... . . \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> d e f ... ]  leftindex 34\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cost-aware idioms in one place, each replacing a hidden O(n) with the right tool:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from bisect import bisect_left\nfrom collections import Counter, deque\nimport heapq\n\ndef unique_in_order(items):\n    \"\"\"Order-preserving de-duplication: O(n) with a set, not O(n^2) with `in list`.\"\"\"\n    seen, out = set(), []\n    for x in items:\n        if x not in seen:\n            seen.add(x)\n            out.append(x)\n    return out\n\ndef bfs_order(graph, start):\n    \"\"\"Breadth-first visiting order: deque.popleft is O(1), list.pop(0) would be O(n).\"\"\"\n    seen, order, queue = {start}, [], deque([start])\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        order.append(node)\n        for nxt in graph.get(node, ()):\n            if nxt not in seen:\n                seen.add(nxt)\n                queue.append(nxt)\n    return order\n\ndef contains_sorted(sorted_items, x):\n    \"\"\"O(log n) membership test in a sorted list.\"\"\"\n    i = bisect_left(sorted_items, x)\n    return i &lt; len(sorted_items) and sorted_items[i] == x\n\ndef report(rows):\n    \"\"\"Build text with one join: O(total length).\"\"\"\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"{name}: {count}\" for name, count in rows)\n\ndef top_words(words, k):\n    \"\"\"k most common words: building the Counter is O(n); most_common(k) uses a heap, O(n log k).\"\"\"\n    return Counter(words).most_common(k)\n\ndef k_smallest(nums, k):\n    return heapq.nsmallest(k, nums)             # O(n log k)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>x in some_list\u003c/code> inside a loop: O(n) per check.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list.insert(0, x)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>del a[0]\u003c/code> used as a queue: use a \u003ccode>deque\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code> in a loop over n items: O(n^2); use a heap, or sort once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>sorted(...)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>min(...)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>len(set(...))\u003c/code> recomputed inside a loop when the data has not changed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>, or on a \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> that something else also references: quadratic. Collect the pieces and \u003ccode>join\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>list(d)[0]\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>list(s)[i]\u003c/code> to read one item: builds an O(n) list first. Use \u003ccode>next(iter(d))\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>Counter.most_common()\u003c/code> with no argument sorts every item, O(n log n); pass k when you need only the top k.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which line makes this O(n^2), and what is the fix? \u003ccode>def dedupe(xs): out = []\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>for x in xs: if x not in out: out.append(x)\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>return out\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>if x not in out\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>out\u003c/code> is a list, so each check scans it, up to n checks of up to n items. Keep a \u003ccode>seen\u003c/code> set beside \u003ccode>out\u003c/code> and test \u003ccode>x not in seen\u003c/code>: O(n) in total, with the order kept.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In CPython, a loop of \u003ccode>s += p\u003c/code> over 4 x 10^5 pieces took about as long as \u003ccode>\"\".join(pieces)\u003c/code>. Why, and why should you still use join?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>CPython notices \u003ccode>s += t\u003c/code> on a \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> that nothing else references and resizes it in place, so the loop is usually linear. The trick fails when another reference to \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> exists (a list holding it, an attribute, a debugger), for \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>, and on implementations such as PyPy, where the loop is quadratic: 0.94 s for only 4 x 10^4 pieces with a second reference in our run. \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> is linear everywhere.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Values arrive one at a time (10^5 of them), and after each arrival you must report how many earlier values are smaller. Why is a list kept sorted with \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> a poor choice, and what are the alternatives?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> shifts up to n references, so 10^5 arrivals cost up to about 5 x 10^9 moves (we measured 2.6 s for 10^5 insorts, against 13 ms for heap pushes). Use \u003ccode>sortedcontainers.SortedList\u003c/code>, whose \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> are fast in practice, or a Fenwick tree over compressed values (the range-query module), both about O(log n) per arrival.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>2215\u003c/b> Find the Difference of Two Arrays: write the version with sets, then swap one set for a list and time both at n = m = 10^4 (use \u003ccode>timeit\u003c/code>). Expected: the set version is O(n + m) and takes a few milliseconds; the list version is O(n x m) and runs hundreds of times slower (seconds).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>557\u003c/b> Reverse Words in a String III: build the result two ways, with one \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> and with \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> on a string. Expected: both look linear in CPython for a local string, for the reason above; only the \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> version is guaranteed O(n). \"deep sea fish\" becomes \"peed aes hsif\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know what Python's containers really charge for each operation, and how to keep hidden O(n) calls out of your loops.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:cost/how-do-you-measure-running-time-and-estimate-the":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Measure with \u003ccode>timeit\u003c/code>: repeat the run and keep the \u003cb>minimum\u003c/b>, because noise only ever adds time. Then time at sizes that double. If T(2n)/T(n) is about 2^b, the code grows like n^b: a ratio of 2 means linear, 4 quadratic, 8 cubic, and a little over 2 means n log n. Fit b across several sizes, predict the next size, and check. Big-O gives the slope; measurement gives the constant, and at small n the constant often decides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Weighing yourself: same scale, same time of day, several readings. One reading tells you little, but how the readings change tells you whether you are gaining. A single timing depends on the machine; how the time changes when n doubles is a property of the code.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Timing correctly\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>time.perf_counter()\u003c/code> is the high-resolution clock for wall time; use it for a single phase of a program.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>timeit\u003c/code> runs a statement many times with garbage collection switched off and returns total times. \u003ccode>Timer.autorange()\u003c/code> picks a loop count that takes at least 0.2 s, and \u003ccode>Timer.repeat()\u003c/code> repeats the measurement; report \u003ccode>min(...) / number\u003c/code>. Noise (other processes, frequency scaling) only adds time, so the minimum is the cleanest estimate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build inputs outside the timed call, use the same inputs for every variant, and keep the machine quiet; laptops on battery throttle.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>The doubling method\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Time the code at n, 2n, 4n and 8n. If T(n) is about a x n^b, then T(2n)/T(n) = 2^b, so \u003cb>b is about log2 of the ratio\u003c/b>. For n log n the ratio is 2(1 + 1/log2 n), a little above 2 (about 2.12 at n = 10^5), so b comes out near 1.1. With four or more sizes, fit a straight line through the points (log n, log T): its slope is b, and it is much less sensitive to one noisy timing than a single ratio. Then predict: T(10n) is about 10^b x T(n). Run the prediction and compare.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>When Big-O misleads\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Constant factors.\u003c/b> Same class, very different constants: \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code> over 10^6 ints in 9 ms against 50 ms for a Python loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small n.\u003c/b> Below a crossover the \"worse\" algorithm wins. We timed \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code> (a C linear scan) against a binary search written in Python, looking up the last element: the scan won up to about 32 to 64 items, while \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code>, which is also in C, won from about 8 to 16 items. Timsort sorts short runs with insertion sort for the same reason.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caches.\u003c/b> Equal operation counts, different memory order: 2 to 4 times apart (the locality card).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Drifting exponents.\u003c/b> At small n fixed overheads flatten the curve (b comes out low); when the data stops fitting in cache, b jumps. Measure over the range of n you actually care about.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input shape.\u003c/b> Sorting 10^6 floats took 29 ms when they were already sorted and 620 ms when random; an adversarial hash pattern or a rare resize can change the picture just as much.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A doubling experiment's own running time is dominated by its largest size: with b = 2 each size takes four times the previous one, so the last size takes about three quarters of the whole experiment. Choose the starting n so that the largest size takes a few seconds. Fitting the line is trivial: a handful of logarithms and one division.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Measure before optimising (to find what actually grows), to check a complexity claim, to choose between two correct solutions in the same class, and to find crossovers for hybrid algorithms. Do not use timings to \u003ci>prove\u003c/i> a bound: noise and caches bend the ratios. Derive the cost by counting, then use the experiment to confirm or refute the prediction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Doubling \u003ccode>count_zero_pairs\u003c/code> (two nested loops over n items, from the lab) on a busy machine:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 500: 12.5 ms. One number alone says nothing about growth\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n          500\ntime      \u003cmark>12.5 ms\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 1,000: 61.6 ms. Ratio 4.9, so b is about log2 4.9 = 2.3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n          500     1,000\ntime      12.5 ms  \u003cmark>61.6 ms\u003c/mark>\nratio               4.9    b ~ 2.3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 2,000: 263 ms. Ratio 4.3, b about 2.1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n          500     1,000     2,000\ntime      12.5 ms  61.6 ms  \u003cmark>263 ms\u003c/mark>\nratio               4.9       4.3   b ~ 2.1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 4,000: 1,194 ms. Ratio 4.5, b about 2.2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n          500     1,000     2,000     4,000\ntime      12.5 ms  61.6 ms  263 ms   \u003cmark>1,194 ms\u003c/mark>\nratio               4.9       4.3       4.5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Fit over all four sizes: b = 2.18. Quadratic, as the count n(n-1)/2 predicts; noise pushed it slightly above 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>predict n = 8,000:  1,194 ms x 2^2.18, about 5.4 s\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Lesson from an earlier run of the same code: one noisy ratio (2.8) suggested b = 1.5. Never conclude from a single pair\u003c/p>\u003cpre>repeat, keep the minimum, fit across sizes\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nimport timeit\n\ndef time_it(fn, repeat=3):\n    \"\"\"Best time of one call to fn(), in seconds, with enough loops to beat timer noise.\"\"\"\n    timer = timeit.Timer(fn)\n    number, _ = timer.autorange()             # loop count that takes at least 0.2 s\n    return min(timer.repeat(repeat=repeat, number=number)) / number\n\ndef doubling(name, make, run, sizes):\n    \"\"\"Time run(make(n)) for doubling n; print ratios, exponent estimates and a fitted exponent.\"\"\"\n    print(f\"\\n{name}\")\n    rows = []\n    for n in sizes:\n        data = make(n)\n        rows.append((n, time_it(lambda: run(data))))\n    for (n0, t0), (n, t) in zip([(None, None)] + rows, rows):\n        extra = f\"  ratio {t / t0:5.2f}  b ~ {math.log2(t / t0):5.2f}\" if t0 else \"\"\n        print(f\"  n = {n:&gt;8,}  {t * 1e3:10.3f} ms{extra}\")\n    xs = [math.log(n) for n, _ in rows]\n    ys = [math.log(t) for _, t in rows]\n    mx, my = sum(xs) / len(xs), sum(ys) / len(ys)\n    b = sum((x - mx) * (y - my) for x, y in zip(xs, ys)) / sum((x - mx) ** 2 for x in xs)\n    print(f\"  fitted exponent b = {b:.2f}\")\n    return b\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Timing the setup.\u003c/b> Building the input inside the timed call measures the wrong thing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One run, or the mean of runs with outliers.\u003c/b> Repeat and keep the minimum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Microsecond timings.\u003c/b> Timer resolution and call overhead dominate; let \u003ccode>autorange\u003c/code> choose the loop count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mutating the input in the timed code.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>a.sort()\u003c/code> sorts \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> on the first call; every later repeat measures the already sorted case.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Concluding from one pair of sizes.\u003c/b> Fit across four or more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing across machines, Python versions or power states.\u003c/b> Compare variants in the same session.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Timings at n = 1,000, 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000 are 0.12, 0.49, 1.95 and 7.9 s. Estimate b and predict n = 16,000.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The ratios are about 4.1, 4.0 and 4.05, so b is about log2 4 = 2: quadratic. Predict 7.9 x 4, about 32 s.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>timeit.repeat(lambda: data.sort(), number=1, repeat=5)\u003c/code> report a misleading minimum for random data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first call sorts \u003ccode>data\u003c/code> in place, so the other four sort an already sorted list, which Timsort finishes with n - 1 comparisons in O(n). The minimum therefore measures the sorted case. Time \u003ccode>sorted(data)\u003c/code>, or rebuild the input for each run.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your O(n log n) solution is slower than a colleague's O(n^2) one on tests where n is at most 50. Is something wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Not necessarily. At n = 50, n^2 is 2,500 basic steps and n log2 n about 280, so constant factors decide: an O(n^2) solution with tiny steps or a C-level inner loop can beat an O(n log n) one with heavy Python steps. Big-O predicts the winner as n grows; measure at the sizes that matter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1588\u003c/b> Sum of All Odd Length Subarrays (stretch): write the O(n^3), O(n^2) (prefix sums) and O(n) (count, for each index, the odd-length subarrays that contain it) versions, stress-test them against each other on random arrays, then time each with \u003ccode>doubling\u003c/code>. Expected: fitted exponents close to 3, 2 and 1; on [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] all three return 57.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1351\u003c/b> Count Negative Numbers in a Sorted Matrix: time your O(mn) and O(m + n) versions on square sorted grids with n = 250 to 2,000. Expected: b near 2 for the brute force and near 1 for the corner walk, and a gap of hundreds of times at n = 2,000.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to measure code honestly, estimate its growth exponent with a doubling experiment, and recognise when Big-O does not predict the winner.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:cost/doubling-experiments-predict-then-measure":"\u003cp>You will predict, then measure, how eight snippets grow: the built-ins you use every day, two classic traps and one surprise. The script takes about two minutes on a typical laptop. Keep the output: later labs reuse the harness.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or later; nothing to install.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Close heavy programs and plug the laptop in, because noise and power saving bend the ratios.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Start a file \u003ccode>doubling.py\u003c/code> with the imports, \u003ccode>time_it\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>doubling\u003c/code> from the Template of the card \"How do you measure running time and estimate the growth exponent?\". Add \u003ccode>import random\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>from collections import deque\u003c/code> to the imports.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Before running anything, copy this table into your notes and fill in the \"Predicted b\" column (0 for constant, 1 for linear, about 1.1 for n log n, 2 for quadratic). Write one line of reasoning for each.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Snippet\u003c/th>\u003cth>Predicted b\u003c/th>\u003cth>Measured b\u003c/th>\u003cth>Explanation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1. \u003ccode>sum(a)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2. \u003ccode>sorted(a)\u003c/code>, random data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3. count pairs with two nested loops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4a. 100 lookups in a list / 4b. in a set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5. n calls of \u003ccode>list.insert(0, x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>6a. drain with \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> / 6b. with \u003ccode>deque.popleft()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>7a. \u003ccode>s += \"ab\"\u003c/code> on a str / 7b. on bytes / 7c. \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8. build a dict from n items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Append the snippets and the main block below to \u003ccode>doubling.py\u003c/code>, and run \u003ccode>python doubling.py\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def rand_list(n):\n    return [random.randrange(-n, n) for _ in range(n)]\n\n\ndef count_zero_pairs(a):\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(a)):\n            if a[i] + a[j] == 0:\n                count += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef lookups(container):\n    return sum(1 for q in range(100) if q in container)\n\n\ndef insert_front(n):\n    a = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        a.insert(0, i)\n\n\ndef drain_list(n):\n    q = list(range(n))\n    while q:\n        q.pop(0)\n\n\ndef drain_deque(n):\n    q = deque(range(n))\n    while q:\n        q.popleft()\n\n\ndef concat(n):\n    s = \"\"\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s += \"ab\"\n    return s\n\n\ndef concat_bytes(n):\n    s = b\"\"\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s += b\"ab\"\n    return s\n\n\ndef join(n):\n    return \"\".join([\"ab\" for _ in range(n)])\n\n\ndef build_dict(a):\n    return {x: i for i, x in enumerate(a)}\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    random.seed(1)\n    big = [100_000, 200_000, 400_000, 800_000]\n    small = [10_000, 20_000, 40_000, 80_000]\n    doubling(\"1. sum(a)\", rand_list, sum, big)\n    doubling(\"2. sorted(a), random data\", rand_list, sorted, big)\n    doubling(\"3. count pairs with two nested loops\", rand_list, count_zero_pairs, [250, 500, 1000, 2000])\n    doubling(\"4a. 100 lookups in a list\", lambda n: list(range(n, 2 * n)), lookups, big)\n    doubling(\"4b. 100 lookups in a set\", lambda n: set(range(n, 2 * n)), lookups, big)\n    doubling(\"5. n calls of list.insert(0, x)\", lambda n: n, insert_front, small)\n    doubling(\"6a. drain with list.pop(0)\", lambda n: n, drain_list, small)\n    doubling(\"6b. drain with deque.popleft()\", lambda n: n, drain_deque, small)\n    doubling(\"7a. s += 'ab' on a str\", lambda n: n, concat, big)\n    doubling(\"7b. s += b'ab' on bytes\", lambda n: n, concat_bytes, [12_500, 25_000, 50_000, 100_000])\n    doubling(\"7c. ''.join(parts)\", lambda n: n, join, big)\n    doubling(\"8. build a dict\", rand_list, build_dict, big)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Copy each fitted exponent into the table. Where the single ratios jump around, run that snippet again and keep the run with the smallest times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain every row where measured and predicted b differ by more than about 0.3. Use the cost sheet and the amortised and locality cards; the questions are: is there a hidden loop, a hidden optimisation, or a cache effect?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change one thing and predict first: time \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>sorted(rand_list(n))\u003c/code> instead of random data, and \u003ccode>x in range(10**9)\u003c/code> instead of a list. Then measure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a three-line journal entry: the biggest surprise, why it happens, and the rule you will apply in your own code.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On a typical laptop with CPython 3.12 (your times will differ; the exponents should not by much):\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>sum, join, deque.popleft, list lookups\u003c/b>: b close to 1. Linear, with C-level constants: \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code> over 800,000 ints takes around 10 milliseconds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>sorted on random data\u003c/b>: b about 1.1, the n log n signature. On already sorted data b is close to 1 and the times are roughly 20 times smaller, because Timsort finds a single run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Set lookups and \u003ccode>x in range\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: b close to 0; the times hardly move with n (ratios bounce around 1 because the work is tiny).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Nested pairs, \u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> and bytes \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: b about 2 at the larger sizes. At the smallest sizes the ratios can be lower than 4, because \u003ccode>memmove\u003c/code> on data that fits in cache is very fast; that is the drifting exponent from the measuring card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>str \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> on a local variable\u003c/b>: b close to 1, not 2. This is CPython's in-place resize for a string with no other references; bytes have no such trick, which is why 7b is quadratic. Do not rely on it (PEP 8).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Building a dict\u003c/b>: b a little above 1 (1.1 to 1.3) at these sizes, because as the table outgrows the caches each insertion costs more cache misses. Still linear in operation count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Noise: on a busy machine single ratios can swing between 1.5 and 3 for a linear snippet. The fitted exponent over four sizes is far more stable, which is the point of fitting.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Snippet 7a measured b = 1.1 but snippet 7b measured b = 2.2. Both build a string of 2n characters by repeated \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code>. Why the difference?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> with no other references, CPython resizes the string in place instead of copying it, so each \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> costs O(length of the piece) amortised. \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code> objects do not get this treatment, so every \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> copies the whole buffer: 2 + 4 + ... + 2n characters in total, Theta(n^2).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1732":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A journey starts at altitude zero. Each entry in a list gives the change in altitude for one successive segment. Return the greatest altitude reached at any point, including the starting point. For \u003ccode>[-3, 5, -1]\u003c/code>, the answer is 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Changes can be negative, zero, or positive. The initial altitude counts even if every later altitude is negative. The input is normally nonempty; the implementation also returns zero for no segments.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You are given the changes between consecutive points, and the question is about the points themselves.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If you knew every altitude, the answer would be a max. Can each altitude be computed from the previous one in constant time, without storing the others?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting operations\u003c/p>\u003cp>The altitude after i steps is the running sum of the first i gains, and the start altitude 0 counts too. Keep the running sum and the best value seen so far in one pass: n additions and comparisons, no list of altitudes.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each stopping point, recompute its altitude by summing all changes from the start, then take the maximum along with zero. Summing overlapping prefixes independently costs O(n²) time and O(1) working space if the maximum is updated immediately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each next altitude is the previous altitude plus the next change. Keep that running altitude and the largest value encountered so far. Initially both are zero. After processing a segment, the running value is its endpoint altitude and the maximum includes precisely the start and all endpoints processed so far, proving the result after the scan.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at zero and take change -3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>altitude = -3, best = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Take changes 5 and -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>altitudes = 2, 1; best = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def highest_altitude(gain):\n    altitude = 0\n    best = 0\n    for change in gain:\n        altitude += change\n        best = max(best, altitude)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The loop performs constant work for each of n changes: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space under constant-cost arithmetic. Large Python integers can add bit-length-dependent arithmetic costs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Initializing the best to the first change omits altitude zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The maximum change is not necessarily the maximum cumulative altitude.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A journey with only downward changes returns zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the earliest index at which the highest altitude occurs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track the lowest altitude as well as the highest during the same scan.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2073":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>People in a queue each want a positive number of tickets. On a turn, the front person buys one ticket and, if still needing tickets, moves to the back. Each purchase takes one unit of time. Return the elapsed time when the person originally at index \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> finishes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ticket counts are positive, and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is a valid original index. Only the target person's completion time is needed, not the queue at every moment. For \u003ccode>[2, 3, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>, the answer is 7.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>People rejoin the back of a queue after each purchase, and you only need the moment one particular person finishes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A second-by-second simulation is correct, but its cost depends on the ticket values, not just n. How many tickets does each other person buy before person k is done?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting operations\u003c/p>\u003cp>Person k finishes on their t[k]-th turn. Anyone at or before k in the line gets min(t[i], t[k]) turns by then; anyone after k gets min(t[i], t[k] - 1), because k's last turn comes before theirs in that round. Summing these is O(n), against about sum(t) steps for the simulation.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Simulate a queue of remaining counts and original indices, buying one ticket per step until k finishes. It uses O(n) space and O(T) time for T purchases before completion. T can be much larger than n when ticket counts are large.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let t be the target's ticket count. Before the target's last purchase, everyone originally at or before k has had up to t opportunities; everyone after k has had only t - 1. People leave as soon as their own demand is met. Thus their contributions are respectively \u003ccode>min(count, t)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>min(count, t - 1)\u003c/code>. These counts partition all purchases through the finishing turn.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Target index 1 needs 3 tickets\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indices 0 and 1 contribute min(2, 3) + min(3, 3) = 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Index 2 is after the target\u003c/p>\u003cpre>it contributes min(2, 2) = 2; total = \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def ticket_time(tickets, k):\n    target = tickets[k]\n    elapsed = 0\n    for i, count in enumerate(tickets):\n        limit = target if i &lt;= k else target - 1\n        elapsed += min(count, limit)\n    return elapsed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. It does not depend on the sum of ticket counts, apart from the bit cost of arithmetic on arbitrarily large integers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A person after k does not get a turn in the target's final round.\u003c/li>\u003cli>People with fewer tickets depart early, so use a minimum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the target needs one ticket, later people contribute zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the completion time for every person, using an appropriate offline counting strategy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Simulate the queue when the actual order of purchases is required.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2215":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two integer lists, return two collections: distinct values appearing only in the first list, and distinct values appearing only in the second. For \u003ccode>[1, 1, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[2, 3]\u003c/code>, the collections are \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code>. Order within each collection is immaterial.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Duplicates in an input do not create duplicate output entries. The two inputs can have different lengths and may be empty. Integer values are hashable, allowing set membership and set difference.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two arrays, values that occur in one but not the other, and duplicates must not repeat in the answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Checking \u003ccode>x not in other_list\u003c/code> for every x hides a loop inside a loop. Which structure answers membership in O(1) and drops duplicates for free?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Python cost sheet\u003c/p>\u003cp>Turn both arrays into sets; the answers are the set differences s1 - s2 and s2 - s1. Building the sets is O(n + m) and each difference is linear in its left operand, against O(n x m) for list membership tests.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + m) expected time, O(n + m) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each value in the first list, scan the second list and append it only if absent there and not already recorded; repeat in the opposite direction. Linear membership checks and duplicate checks can take O((n + m)²) time, with O(n + m) space for the result.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A set removes duplicates while preserving membership information. The set difference \u003ccode>a - b\u003c/code> contains precisely values present in a and absent from b; reversing the operands gives the other collection. Converting the differences to lists satisfies the return shape without imposing an order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Create sets for [1, 1, 2] and [2, 3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first = {1, 2}, second = {2, 3}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Subtract in both directions\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first - second = {1}; second - first = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def distinct_differences(first, second):\n    a = set(first)\n    b = set(second)\n    return [list(a - b), list(b - a)]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building sets and computing both differences takes O(n + m) expected time and O(n + m) space, including the output, assuming expected constant-time hashing. Adversarial collisions can worsen the running time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A list membership test inside a loop can silently introduce quadratic work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning every occurrence instead of distinct values violates the contract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Set iteration order is not a promised output order; compare answers as sets.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort each result if a deterministic ascending order is required.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the values common to both inputs with set intersection.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:557":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reverse the characters inside each word while keeping the words in their original order and preserving spaces. For \u003ccode>\"ab cd e\"\u003c/code>, return \u003ccode>\"ba dc e\"\u003c/code>. A space is the separator; the method below also preserves repeated or edge spaces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Strings are immutable in Python, so individual character assignments are unavailable. Using the literal single-space separator rather than the default whitespace split preserves the number and positions of spaces. Empty input is also handled.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every word is transformed independently and the spaces stay where they are.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Strings are immutable, so every concatenation builds a new string. How can you produce each reversed word and assemble the result with linear total work?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Python cost sheet\u003c/p>\u003cp>Split on single spaces, reverse each word with a slice, and join the pieces with one \u003ccode>\" \".join(...)\u003c/code>. Each character is copied a constant number of times, so the work is O(n); appending character by character with \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> is only linear thanks to a fragile CPython optimisation.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space for the output and the pieces\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan each word backwards and repeatedly concatenate individual characters to a result string. This is straightforward, but immutable concatenation can repeatedly copy an expanding prefix, giving O(n²) time in a general cost model. It also needs O(n) space for the output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Splitting on \u003ccode>\" \"\u003c/code> produces every word and an empty piece for each extra separator. Reverse each piece using a slice, then join with the same separator. Each non-space character remains in its original word and appears in reverse order, while joining restores exactly the separators between pieces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Split \"ab  cd\" on single spaces\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pieces = [\"ab\", \"\", \"cd\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse each piece and join\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[\"ba\", \"\", \"dc\"] becomes \u003cmark>\"ba  dc\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def reverse_words(text):\n    return \" \".join(piece[::-1] for piece in text.split(\" \"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Splitting, slicing, and joining copy O(n) characters in total, so time and space are O(n), including the output and intermediate pieces. This does not rely on implementation-specific optimizations for repeated string concatenation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>split()\u003c/code> without an explicit separator collapses whitespace and loses edge spaces.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reversing the entire string also reverses word order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Python slices reverse Unicode code points, not necessarily user-perceived grapheme clusters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse word order instead of characters within each word.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a grapheme-aware library if visual characters rather than code points must be reversed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:119":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return row \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> of the triangle whose first row is \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code> and whose later rows have boundary values 1 and interior values equal to the sum of the two neighboring entries above. Rows are numbered from zero: row 3 is \u003ccode>[1, 3, 3, 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The index k is nonnegative. Only one row is requested, so retaining every earlier row is unnecessary. The answer has k + 1 integers; integer values may grow large.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only one row of a triangle is wanted, and each row is built from the previous one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You never need rows older than the previous one. Can a single list of length k + 1 hold the previous row and become the next row, if you choose the order of updates carefully?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Space accounting\u003c/p>\u003cp>Append a 1, then update \u003ccode>row[j] += row[j - 1]\u003c/code> for j from right to left. Going right to left, \u003ccode>row[j - 1]\u003c/code> still holds the previous row's value when row[j] needs it, exactly the rolling-array invariant. k rows of up to k updates: O(k^2) time in O(k) space.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(k^2) time, O(k) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build and retain every row from zero through k, deriving each new row from the preceding one. The rows contain a total of O(k²) entries, so this takes O(k²) time and O(k²) space, including all retained rows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep one row and append the new right boundary. Update interior positions from right to left: at position j, both \u003ccode>row[j]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>row[j - 1]\u003c/code> still contain values from the previous row. Their sum is the required new interior value. The left boundary remains 1, so induction gives the correct row after each iteration.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start with row 0 and build row 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[1] -&gt; [1, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build rows 2 and 3, updating from the right\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[1, 2, 1] -&gt; [1, 3, 3, \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def pascal_row(k):\n    row = [1]\n    for length in range(1, k + 1):\n        row.append(1)\n        for j in range(length - 1, 0, -1):\n            row[j] += row[j - 1]\n    return row\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(k²) additions and O(k) space for the returned row, with O(1) additional variables. This counts additions as constant cost; Python's large-integer arithmetic grows with coefficient bit length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Updating left to right reuses a value already changed for the new row.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Row zero is \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>, not an empty row.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not overwrite either boundary with an interior sum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Build every row if the complete triangle is needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Derive successive binomial coefficients using exact multiplication and division.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1656":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a stream with positions 1 through n. Values arrive once each, in arbitrary position order. After inserting a value, return the longest consecutive run of already-arrived values starting at the next position not yet returned. Advance that next position past the run.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each position is valid and inserted exactly once; values are strings. An insert can release several previously stored values or none. \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> is reserved internally as an unfilled marker, so even an empty string works as a value.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Items arrive out of order and you must release the longest run of consecutive items that is ready, starting from the next one owed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One insert can release many values, so a single call is not O(1). What is the total work over all n inserts if the \"next owed\" index never moves backwards?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Amortised analysis\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store each value in an array at its id and keep a pointer to the next id owed. On each insert, advance the pointer while the slot is filled, collecting values. The pointer moves forward at most n times over the whole run, so n inserts cost O(n) in total: O(1) amortised each.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) total time for n inserts (O(1) amortised per insert), O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store arrivals in a dictionary. On each insertion, search all stored positions for the next releasable one and continue until finding a gap. Repeated full searches can cost O(n²) over all insertions and require O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An array indexes directly by position, and a pointer identifies the first value not yet emitted. After storing an arrival, walk forward while the pointer's slot is filled. Every emitted slot is consecutive and begins at the pointer; stopping at the first missing slot prevents skipping. The pointer never retreats, so across all calls it advances at most n times.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert position 2 before position 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slots = [empty, \"b\"]; next = 1; output = []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert position 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>emit \"a\", then \"b\"; next = 3; output = \u003cmark>\"a\", \"b\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class OrderedStream:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.slots = [None] * (n + 1)\n        self.next = 1\n\n    def insert(self, position, value):\n        self.slots[position] = value\n        chunk = []\n        while self.next &lt; len(self.slots) and self.slots[self.next] is not None:\n            chunk.append(self.slots[self.next])\n            self.next += 1\n        return chunk\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Initialization takes O(n) time and space. One insert can take O(n) time when it releases a large chunk, but all n inserts together take O(n), or O(1) amortized per insert, counting returned entries. A chunk uses space proportional to its length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Checking truthiness would incorrectly treat an empty string as unfilled.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not move past a missing position, even if later positions are present.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Duplicate or out-of-range inserts are outside this contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Support repeated updates by defining whether previously emitted values can be revised.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a sparse map if the possible position range is huge relative to arrivals.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1351":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count negative entries in a rectangular matrix whose rows and columns are each sorted in non-increasing order. For \u003ccode>[[3, 0, -2], [1, -1, -3]]\u003c/code>, return 3. Zero is not negative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The row and column monotonicity both matter: moving right or down cannot increase a value. The usual input is nonempty and rectangular; this implementation also accepts an empty matrix or rows of length zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A grid sorted in non-increasing order along every row and every column, and a count of values below zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Brute force is O(mn), and a binary search per row gives O(m log n). Start at a corner where one direction makes values smaller and the other makes them larger. What does one comparison there tell you about a whole row or column?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Asymptotic bounds and cases\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start at the bottom-left. If the value is negative, every value to its right in that row is negative too, so add (n - col) and move up a row; otherwise move one column right. Every step discards a row or a column, so there are at most m + n steps.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Visit every cell and increment a counter when the value is below zero. This takes O(mn) time and O(1) working space for m rows and n columns. It is an independent way to check small examples.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start at the bottom-left. If the current cell is negative, all cells to its right in the same row are also negative; count them and move up, leaving the counted row. Otherwise this cell and all cells above it in the same column are nonnegative, so move right and discard that column. Each move eliminates an unprocessed row or column, and the two cases count every negative exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at bottom-left, value 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>it is nonnegative; move right to -1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Count the bottom row suffix, then inspect the upper row\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 2, move up; 0 is nonnegative, move right\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Count the upper suffix\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 1; total = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_negatives(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return 0\n    row = len(grid) - 1\n    col = 0\n    count = 0\n    width = len(grid[0])\n    while row &gt;= 0 and col &lt; width:\n        if grid[row][col] &lt; 0:\n            count += width - col\n            row -= 1\n        else:\n            col += 1\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most m upward and n rightward moves occur, so time is O(m + n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The empty checks are constant time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Treating zero as negative overcounts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Row sorting alone does not justify discarding the column above a nonnegative cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Counting only the current negative cell misses its entire rightward suffix.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Binary-search the first negative in each row in O(m log n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count entries below a different threshold by changing the comparison.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1588":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sum the sums of all contiguous odd-length subarrays of an integer list. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>, the odd-length subarrays are the three singletons and the whole list, giving \u003ccode>1 + 2 + 3 + 6 = 12\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every eligible subarray contributes its elements, including overlapping occurrences. A direct enumeration considers quadratically many ranges. The contribution argument works for negative or zero integers too, and an empty input produces zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A sum over all subarrays of odd length, with a follow-up asking for linear time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Instead of enumerating subarrays, ask how many times each a[i] is counted. How many subarrays contain index i, and how many of those have odd length?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting operations\u003c/p>\u003cp>Index i is in (i + 1) x (n - i) subarrays (choose a start at or before i and an end at or after i). The odd-length ones are ((i + 1) x (n - i) + 1) // 2 of them, because start and end positions split almost evenly between equal and different parities. Summing a[i] times that count is O(n), against O(n^3) for brute force and O(n^2) with prefix sums.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate each start and end, and for odd lengths explicitly sum the selected slice. There are O(n²) ranges and each sum can take O(n), for O(n³) time and O(1) working space if sums are computed by loops without slicing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For index i, there are \u003ccode>i + 1\u003c/code> choices of left endpoint and \u003ccode>n - i\u003c/code> choices of right endpoint. The length is odd exactly when the number of positions chosen on each side has matching parity. Among the product of those choices, exactly \u003ccode>(product + 1) // 2\u003c/code> pairs have matching parity: each side alternates parity and starts with an even distance. Multiply that count by the value and sum over indices; swapping the summation order preserves the total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For [1, 2, 3], count odd ranges containing each index\u003c/p>\u003cpre>products = [3, 4, 3]; odd counts = [2, 2, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add the contributions\u003c/p>\u003cpre>1*2 + 2*2 + 3*2 = \u003cmark>12\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def odd_subarray_sum(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total = 0\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        choices = (i + 1) * (n - i)\n        total += value * ((choices + 1) // 2)\n    return total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One pass takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space under constant-cost integer arithmetic. Python's unbounded integer operations can cost more as values grow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count position occurrences, not distinct values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use integer division after adding one; ordinary division yields a float.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Counting all containing subarrays without restricting parity overcounts.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count contributions from even-length ranges instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use prefix sums to enumerate all odd ranges in O(n²) time as a second oracle.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1381":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Implement a bounded stack with three operations: push a value if there is room, pop and return the top value or \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> if empty, and add an increment to each of the bottom \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> current elements. A push to a full stack is ignored. For example, pushing 2 and 5, incrementing the bottom two by 3, then popping yields 8 followed by 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The capacity and k are nonnegative integers. Increment values and stored values are integers; increments can be negative. If k exceeds the current size, every existing element is incremented. The operation must affect elements present at that moment, not values pushed later. The empty-stack sentinel is \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code>, even though a valid stored value might also equal \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A stack with an operation that changes the bottom k elements, where a loop over k items per call is too slow for the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Do you need the incremented values now, or only when each element is popped? Where could a pending increment wait so that pops can pick it up and pass it on?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Amortised analysis\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep an array \u003ccode>inc\u003c/code> beside the stack. \u003ccode>increment(k, val)\u003c/code> adds val to \u003ccode>inc[min(k, size) - 1]\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>stack[top] + inc[top]\u003c/code>, then adds \u003ccode>inc[top]\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>inc[top - 1]\u003c/code> (the increment still applies to everything below) and clears it. Every operation is O(1) in the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(maxSize) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Represent the stack with a list. A push or pop is constant time amortized for Python's list, while increment loops over the first \u003ccode>min(k, size)\u003c/code> elements. This uses O(capacity) space and can take O(capacity) time for a single increment. Repeating large increments before any pops repeats work unnecessarily.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Record a deferred increment at the highest affected index instead of updating every element. Interpret \u003ccode>pending[j]\u003c/code> as an increment owed to all currently present positions from zero through j. The value owed to a particular position is the sum of pending entries at that position and above. On pop, the top receives its pending increment; propagate that amount one position down so the remaining affected positions still receive it. A new push starts with zero pending, so it cannot inherit increments issued before it existed. Each operation preserves this representation and does constant work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push 2 and 5, then increment bottom 2 by 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values = [2, 5], pending = [0, 3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop the top and propagate its pending amount\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return 8; values = [2], pending = [3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>; stack is empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class CustomStack:\n    def __init__(self, max_size):\n        self.max_size = max_size\n        self.values = []\n        self.pending = []\n\n    def push(self, value):\n        if len(self.values) &lt; self.max_size:\n            self.values.append(value)\n            self.pending.append(0)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self.values:\n            return -1\n        extra = self.pending.pop()\n        value = self.values.pop()\n        if self.pending:\n            self.pending[-1] += extra\n        return value + extra\n\n    def increment(self, k, value):\n        count = min(k, len(self.values))\n        if count:\n            self.pending[count - 1] += value\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each operation performs O(1) work, with O(capacity) maximum space. Python list append and pop at the end are amortized O(1), so the push/pop bound in this implementation is amortized, not a strict worst-case guarantee; increment is O(1) worst-case under constant-cost arithmetic. A fixed-size array and explicit size would avoid resizing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Updating the top pending entry rather than the last affected entry changes too many values when k is small.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Failing to propagate on pop loses an increment owed to lower elements.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Incrementing an empty stack or using k = 0 does nothing; a later push must not receive that old increment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Negative k is outside the stated contract, and \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> alone cannot distinguish an empty pop from a stored \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use preallocated arrays to make push and pop worst-case constant time under the fixed-width model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Design a structure supporting increments on arbitrary intervals rather than prefixes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
